# AGENTS.md

Briefing for AI agents (and new contributors) working in this Odoo project.
It was generated by `odx new`; edit it as the project grows.

- **Project**: {{project_name}}
- **Odoo version**: {{version}}
- **Tooling**: the `odx` CLI drives everything below — prefer it over calling
  `odoo-bin`, `pip` or `psql` by hand, so the config and addons path stay in sync.

## Layout

```
compose.yml        PostgreSQL for local development (Docker Compose)
odoo.conf          Committed base config
odoo.conf.local    Generated, git-ignored; odx rewrites its addons_path — do not hand-edit
src/odoo/          Vanilla upstream Odoo {{version}} — read it, never modify it
custom_addons/     Modules written for this project (your work goes here)
external_addons/   Third-party modules (OCA, vendors) — treat as read-only
.venv/             Python environment used by every odx command
.testing/sessions/ Run and test logs (see "Where the logs are")
```

Never patch Odoo core under `src/odoo/`. If core behavior needs to change, override
it from a module in `custom_addons/` (inheritance, `_inherit`, monkey-patch in
`__init__.py` as a last resort) so the checkout stays a clean upstream clone.

## Commands

```bash
odx db start                  # start PostgreSQL (docker compose)
odx run                       # run the server (live log dashboard)
odx run --plain               # plain streamed logs; use this when you need pdb
odx shell -d <db>             # Odoo shell against a database
odx update -d <db>            # update all modules
odx update-module <m> -d <db> # update one module
odx test [<tags>...]          # run tests in a throwaway database
odx i18n -d <db> [-m <mod>] [--lang es_BO]   # export .pot/.po per addon
odx install                   # (re)install Python dependencies into .venv
odx clean                     # drop __pycache__/*.pyc/*.log, prune old run sessions
odx doctor                    # check system dependencies
odx db ls|psql|logs|stop      # database utilities
odx db drop <db> [--force]    # delete a database
```

Global flags worth knowing: `--json` (machine-readable output, no prompts/colors),
`--quiet` (errors and final results only), `--no-progress`, `--color never`.

## Running the server

`odx run` opens a live dashboard: `q` quit, `r` restart odoo-bin, `/` search,
`l` cycle the level filter, `g`/`G` top/bottom, arrows or `j`/`k` scroll, Ctrl+C quits
from anywhere.

`r` stops odoo-bin (gracefully, then forcefully) and starts it again without leaving
the dashboard — the log scrollback, filter and search are kept, and everything lands
in the same `run.log`. The addons path is rebuilt on each restart, so this is the way
to pick up a **newly created module** in `custom_addons/`, a change to `odoo.conf`, or
a `--dev=reload` that stopped reacting. Restarting does not touch the database: use
`odx update-module <m> -d <db>` after changing models, views or data files.

The dashboard enables `--dev=reload,qweb,werkzeug,xml`. It deliberately leaves out
`pdb`, which needs an interactive stdin the dashboard cannot provide — run
`odx run --plain` when you want the post-mortem debugger.

## Testing

```bash
odx test                                  # all tests of every module in custom_addons
odx test /my_module                       # one module
odx test /my_module:TestClass.test_method # one test
```

`odx test` creates a temporary database, installs the modules under test, runs them
in a single `odoo-bin` invocation and drops the database afterwards — including on
failure or Ctrl+C. Write tests with `TransactionCase`/`HttpCase` and tag them so they
can be selected individually.

## Where the logs are

- `.testing/sessions/run-<timestamp>/run.log` — full, unfiltered log of one `odx run`
  (written even when the dashboard filters what is on screen).
- `.testing/sessions/<run_id>/` — one `odx test` session: `combined.log`,
  `warnings.log`, `runs/*.log`, `report.json`, plus a `latest` symlink.

Read these instead of re-running a long command to see what happened. `odx clean`
keeps the five most recent `run-*` sessions and leaves test sessions alone.

## Module conventions

- One concern per module; technical names in `snake_case`.
- Required files: `__init__.py`, `__manifest__.py`, `security/ir.model.access.csv`
  for every new model.
- Code, identifiers and comments in English; user-facing strings go through `_()`
  and are translated in `i18n/*.po`.
- Keep `depends` minimal and accurate — it defines install and upgrade order.
- XML data files: use explicit external IDs, and `noupdate="1"` for records users
  are expected to edit.

Check the view syntax for **Odoo {{version}}** before writing XML: `<list>` replaced
`<tree>` in 18.0, and `attrs`/`states` were removed in 17.0 in favor of direct
`invisible`/`readonly`/`required` attributes with a Python expression.

## Before you call a change done

1. `odx test /<module>` passes.
2. `odx update-module <module> -d <db>` applies cleanly on an existing database.
3. New models have access rules; new user-facing strings are translatable.
4. No changes under `src/odoo/`, and no secrets in `odoo.conf` or committed files.
